WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.120
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:07.475
<v Examiner>Name and describe the four processes of pharmacokinetics that an oral medicine goes

3
00:00:07.475 --> 00:00:09.282
<v Examiner>through once it's swallowed.

4
00:00:09.732 --> 00:00:13.241
Pharmacokinetics is what the body does to the drug.

5
00:00:13.241 --> 00:00:18.884
Four processes, A D M E, one mark each, and this is close to her own model answer.

6
00:00:19.334 --> 00:00:24.549
Absorption: the drug moves into the bloodstream, mostly from the small intestine.

7
00:00:24.549 --> 00:00:29.700
Distribution: it travels around the body in the blood, bound to plasma proteins.

8
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:35.044
Metabolism: the liver's enzymes change it, to make it water-soluble and less toxic.

9
00:00:35.044 --> 00:00:40.710
Elimination, or excretion: it leaves the body, mostly in the urine, through the kidneys.

10
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.827
Three related terms for the multi-choice.

11
00:00:43.827 --> 00:00:49.618
First-pass metabolism: a drug taken by mouth goes through the liver before it reaches the

12
00:00:49.618 --> 00:00:53.131
circulation, and a lot of it can be broken down there.

13
00:00:53.131 --> 00:00:57.490
So a drug with very high first-pass metabolism is not given orally.

14
00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:01.003
I V, sublingual and the other non-oral routes skip it.

15
00:01:01.003 --> 00:01:05.818
Dissolution is the tablet dissolving in the gut fluids to form a solution,

16
00:01:05.818 --> 00:01:07.509
before it can be absorbed.

17
00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:13.105
And half-life is the time it takes for the amount of drug in the body to drop by half.

18
00:01:13.555 --> 00:01:17.328
Pharmacokinetics, what the body does to the drug, A D M E.

19
00:01:17.328 --> 00:01:20.842
Absorption into the blood, mostly the small intestine.

20
00:01:20.842 --> 00:01:24.225
Distribution in the blood, bound to plasma proteins.

21
00:01:24.225 --> 00:01:27.478
Metabolism in the liver, to make it water-soluble.

22
00:01:27.478 --> 00:01:29.755
Elimination, mostly by the kidneys.

23
00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:33.204
First-pass: the liver breaks down an oral drug first.

24
00:01:33.204 --> 00:01:35.741
Half-life: time for the level to halve.
